#
# LED-detection hook for intel-mips.
#
# The four status LEDs need nothing here: the generic diag.sh resolves
# boot/running/failsafe/upgrade from the led-boot/led-running/
# led-failsafe/led-upgrade aliases in each board's DT and writes to
# /sys/class/leds by name. Only LEDs that need a uci-level trigger belong
# in this file.
#
# The wifi LED is the one alias diag.sh does not resolve, which is easy to
# get wrong: it reads those four names and no more, so a led-wifi alias
# does nothing whatsoever until a board.d file hands it to
# ucidef_set_led_wlan. The call below is that handover. It sits outside
# the case, the shape the lantiq target uses, because the alias means the
# same thing wherever it appears: a board opts in by declaring it in its
# DT rather than by being named here.

. /lib/functions.sh
. /lib/functions/leds.sh
. /lib/functions/uci-defaults.sh

board_config_update

#
# The name is read back out of the DT instead of being written out again
# here. Both ends compose it from the same properties -- the kernel at
# registration, get_dt_led from /proc/device-tree -- and a mismatch
# between them is silent, because led_set_attr only writes when the sysfs
# file already exists. Deriving it from the alias removes the chance of
# the two drifting apart, and it spans both spellings this target
# contains without having to know which board uses which: the 7560
# names its LEDs with a legacy label, the 7580 and 7590 with colour
# and function.
#
# A board with no led-wifi alias yields an empty string, hence the guard.
# Without it ucidef_set_led_wlan would write a board.json entry keyed on
# nothing.
#
# phy0tpt is the throughput trigger of whichever radio registered first,
# not of a nominated band: mac80211 builds the trigger name from
# wiphy_name() at the point the driver creates it, so the index is kernel
# probe order and owes nothing to the ucidef_add_wlan order in 02_network.
# On all three boards that first radio is the 5 GHz one -- on the 7580
# and 7590 because both parts are ath10k and PCI domain 0 enumerates
# ahead of domain 1, on the 7560 because the QCA9882's ath10k_pci
# probes well before the AR93xx's ath9k, which comes up as phy1. One lamp
# cannot follow two radios in any case, and taking the first one leaves
# it working on whichever radio survives if the other fails to register.
#
# Worth knowing before copying this to another target: ath10k only has a
# tpt trigger because OpenWrt adds one, in
# 930-ath10k_add_tpt_led_trigger.patch, under CPTCFG_MAC80211_LEDS --
# which the mac80211 package turns on whenever CONFIG_LEDS_TRIGGERS is
# set. Mainline ath10k registers no mac80211 LED trigger at all, and
# writing a name that no trigger answers to fails quietly.
#
led_wifi="$(get_dt_led wifi)"
[ -n "$led_wifi" ] && ucidef_set_led_wlan "wifi" "wifi" "$led_wifi" "phy0tpt"

case "$(board_name)" in
avm,fritz7560)
	#
	# The "Fon" LED follows the wan interface. The panel has no
	# Internet LED to use for this — AVM's uplink was DSL and its state
	# shares the Power LED — and Fon is the one front-panel lamp this
	# port has no use of its own for, since no telephony support is
	# planned. The 7560 has no WAN jack either, so the shipped
	# default declares all four front-panel sockets as LAN and no wan
	# interface exists out of the box; the mapping is therefore inert
	# until one LAN port is nominated as WAN at runtime, at which point
	# the LED starts working with no image change. That is intended.
	#
	ucidef_set_led_netdev "wan" "WAN" "green:fon" "wan"
	;;
avm,fritz7580)
	#
	# This board is the one of the three with a lamp that already means
	# what the trigger says, so it does not borrow Fon the way the other
	# two do: AVM's device tree gives SSO pin 6 the name "internet", and
	# an Internet lamp following the wan interface is the mapping the
	# silkscreen already promises. Fon is left alone here as a result.
	#
	# Unlike the 7560 and like the 7590, this board has a real WAN
	# jack behind the fifth GSWIP port, so 02_network declares a wan
	# interface out of the box and the lamp is live from first boot
	# rather than waiting for a LAN port to be nominated at runtime.
	#
	# The name is coupled to the DTS: it is composed from the LED node's
	# colour and function, so this string has to track
	# seale_avm_fritz7580.dts's SSO pin-6 child. A mismatch is silent —
	# board.json gets an entry keyed on a LED that does not exist and
	# nothing ever writes to it.
	#
	# The lamp pin 6 drives is named from AVM's own 4.9 board file, which
	# calls it "internet"; the panel of a 7580 carries such a lamp. The
	# DTS follows that file and so does this line. Were a name ever to
	# turn out wrong, the fix is a rename here and in the DTS.
	#
	ucidef_set_led_netdev "wan" "WAN" "green:internet" "wan"
	;;
avm,fritz7590)
	#
	# The wifi lamp is handled above and needs nothing special here even
	# though it hangs off the SSO controller rather than off a GPIO: a
	# populated /sys/class/leds/<name>/ is the whole contract, and
	# leds-lgm-sso registers the same class devices gpio-leds would.
	#
	# Fon follows the wan interface, the trigger the 7560 takes above --
	# and here there is a real WAN jack behind it rather than a LAN port
	# nominated at runtime, so it works out of the box. Fon is the lamp
	# this port has no use of its own for, telephony being unsupported on
	# both boards.
	#
	# Which silkscreened lamp this actually is has not been confirmed
	# against the panel; if it turns out wrong, the fix is a rename here
	# and in the DTS and nothing more.
	#
	ucidef_set_led_netdev "wan" "WAN" "green:fon" "wan"
	;;
esac

board_config_flush

exit 0
